WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:09.990 align:start position:0%
 
head forester  andreas palmer is conducting an  experiment for his guests  imagine we're all little trees we're all  spruce saplings all close together just  like in nature  and because we're trees we don't just  want to grow up we want to spread our  branches too  so let's all stand up and spread our  arms and  you see how tight it gets lesson number  one  in nature the stronger one prevails  always  the problem here is too many spruces and  too few silver furs  the forester explains the fur's benefits  you see what a fat root this is like a  carrot he says  it illustrates well that these trees can  reach more different minerals in the  ground  deeper water reserves in the summer  making them simply the more stable trees  they're comparable with oak trees on the  flat plains  the oak epitomizes sturdiness in lowland  forests  and up here it's the fur  ten women and four men are spending a  week outdoors to help the forest and  protect the valuable furs  only a few grow as tall as these  young furs are a delicacy for deers and  stags that wander through  nibbling off the buds stunting their  growth says andreas palmer  and that puts other trees at an  advantage  and where furs and spruces coexist  animals seem to always prefer eating the  furs  there is no vacation these volunteers  have booked a week of hard forestry work  one participant says they're going to  learn how to wrap the furs in wool  that's supposed to protect young  saplings against being bitten off  but before work can start more  instructions  the helpers are getting chilly and  impatient but the chief forester wants  to make sure  everyone knows how it's done right  here  looks easy but it's not even finding the  baby furs in the forest is a problem  the rule of thumb is spruce's sting furs  don't  project manager andreas friesica  observes and criticizes  that's not going to work he tells ronnie  the volunteer  the sapling might not be bitten off but  there's so much wool it won't grow next  season  use less and ronnie asks even less than  that  carolyn says she loves it here it's  fascinating  it's a lot of work but she thinks it's  great to achieve something through  simple means  stopping the deer from coming and  destroying the trees  i find their enthusiasm for each tiny  tree astonishing  but unlike the volunteers this is my  first time here  everyone in the group takes up the  burden once a year to work for germany's  forests  yesterday at the train station at johann  georgenstadt in saxony  it was all a big welcome back these  people know each other  weeks in the woods together forge bonds  some are here for the eighth time  participants pay  only for travel here explains project  leader andreas friesica  room and board are free anyone should be  able to join  forestry authorities cover 40 of the  costs the rest  come from donations and sponsors  andreas friesica welcomes everyone to  their lodgings on behalf of the project  he says when they go in they can decide  among themselves who will sleepwear the  accommodation is on the level of a youth  hostel  with several people to a room pure  luxury says the team  i notice a hot water bottle cecelia  laughs and says  just in case but the rooms seem to be  plenty warm here  next morning 6 a.m preparing to plant  fur saplings  these outsized tools are called mattocks  they're needed to cut a proper hole in  the ground for planting  each one weighs four kilos in the course  of the day  they easily feel much heavier  the day's objective is ambitious  500 plantings says andreas we should  make it but we'll see  the rest get put into moist soil  together so they don't dry out by  tomorrow  the official objective for the week is  to plant two thousand  new silver furs the forester has  only five employees covering a huge area  so he's glad for the extra help  these tiny furs are already three years  old  now care is needed the roots have to  stay undamaged  most of these saplings won't survive for  every 25  thirds that are planted only one on  average makes it  the project leader says it all depends  on how much wildlife there is  and on other factors the amount of  snowfall how dry the summer is  whether other trees will fall on it  there are a whole lot of factors that  influence whether a little sapling  like this grows to maturity  i'm surprised at how bad the odds slowly  i begin to understand why the crew were  taking so much care with the wool  earlier on  back in the woods chief forrester palmer  introduces the crew to the craft of  planting trees  the matic is an unwieldy tool but  carolyn gets the hang of it  together she a teacher and ronnie a  truck driver  are a dream team  dig out the hole plant the fur  and check for stability all in neat rows  with a meter and a half of space in  between  i ask carolyn what's the hardest part of  planting  she says it's making the hole because  usually there's some kind of surprise  involved  a rock or a root  here it gets tough as straight as  possible as deep as possible  and losing as little soil as possible  keeps the troops fed while they toil  away he's responsible for a hot lunch  not so easy out here in the wild  first he gets only smoke but then the  flames catch  carolyn and ronnie have developed a real  rhythm their furs  stand proud  berlin native ronnie says nothing beats  nature  and if there's anything he can do for it  it's here with the forest project  lunchtime finally comes at 2 30 but the  break  is a short one reaching today's goal of  500 plantings  is looking pretty unlikely  but they go for it anyway  anyone who can and wants to can work  alone quality remains top priority  the volunteers are slower than  professional forestry workers  but they compensate through their  motivation and the sheer fact they're  here  ninety percent of their plants take root  the professionals work in pairs and  manage only 65 percent  bianca says it's hard work getting the  ground ready but it's also very nice  work  why is that i ask she says she was  telling wilfred earlier  it's like building a nest for these baby  trees  she doesn't know whether their efforts  will produce results  by now the matics feel very heavy indeed  elisa and cecilia are having a tough  time of it  what's the problem i ask cecilia says  it's a giant nugget  elisa says the rock has to be removed  but it's stuck  it's become a personal struggle for them  i'd have started digging elsewhere  long ago  finally elisa says they've got the right  technique  i ask is it fun or hard it's both they  agree  but it's not enough for the project  leader darkness falls in an hour  there's still plenty of plants to go and  they're all so puny  andrea says each sapling gets a shock  from being replanted and it has to  adjust to its new environment  that weakens the plant it's called  planting  shock and it depends how big the shock  is  when it goes into the ground weakened  the likelihood it will survive the shock  is even lower  the ground here is simply too tough  uneven and full of rocks and roots  no easy job for beginners by evening 160  saplings are left unplanted  the kitchen crew is waiting for the  volunteers return  dinner is all organic to fortify the  workers for tomorrow  they're here another four days as their  good deed  for the forest

